# 6. FOUR AI AGENTS + HUMAN REVIEW

This is the central workflow of the application.

The application must have FOUR AI agents and ONE separate Human Reviewer.

The Human Reviewer is NOT an AI agent.

Create a clear visual Five-Stage Workflow.

## STAGE 01 — ORCHESTRATOR AGENT

Role: Workflow Manager.

Responsibilities:

- Coordinate the entire knowledge lifecycle.
- Start a knowledge processing run.
- Validate uploaded documents.
- Check required configurations.
- Create an execution plan.
- Assign tasks to other agents.
- Track agent execution.
- Maintain processing state.
- Handle errors.
- Retry transient failed operations only once where appropriate.
- Log every important action.

Show:
- Run ID.
- Current processing stage.
- Execution status.
- Start time.
- Completion time.
- Call usage where available.
- Error messages.

The Orchestrator must ensure stages execute in the correct order.

## STAGE 02 — KNOWLEDGE INTAKE AND ORGANIZER AGENT

Role: Document Understanding and Classification.

Responsibilities:

1. Receive uploaded institutional documents.
2. Extract text from PDF files.
3. Extract text from DOCX files.
4. Read TXT files.
5. Support CSV or other structured files where relevant.
6. Identify document language.
7. Identify the document category.
8. Extract document title.
9. Extract effective date.
10. Extract expiry or review date when available.
11. Identify department.
12. Detect duplicate documents.
13. Identify potentially outdated versions.
14. Divide document content into meaningful chunks.
15. Generate semantic embeddings.
16. Save document metadata and searchable content.

Supported education document categories:

- Admission Policies.
- Fee Structures.
- Scholarship Policies.
- Academic Calendars.
- Examination Rules.
- Attendance Policies.
- Course Information.
- Timetables.
- Student Handbooks.
- Faculty Guidelines.
- Library Rules.
- Hostel Policies.
- Transport Information.
- Campus Rules.
- Student Affairs.
- IT Guidelines.

Allow administrators to create custom categories.

Important:
If a PDF is scanned and text cannot be extracted, identify it as requiring OCR. Do not pretend the document was successfully processed.

## STAGE 03 — KNOWLEDGE CURATOR AND AUTHOR AGENT

Role: Knowledge Creation.

After the Organizer completes its task, this agent receives the extracted and classified information.

Responsibilities:

- Convert institutional information into structured knowledge.
- Generate clear knowledge articles.
- Create frequently asked questions.
- Create short summaries.
- Organize rules and procedures.
- Preserve original dates, numbers, conditions, and policy wording.
- Add department and category information.
- Link every article to its original source document.
- Include page or section references where available.
- Identify information that requires clarification.

Example:

Uploaded Document:
University Admission Policy 2026.

Generated Knowledge Article:

Title:
"University Admission Requirements 2026"

Sections:
- Eligibility Criteria.
- Required Documents.
- Application Procedure.
- Application Deadline.
- Fee Information.
- Contact Department.
- Original Source References.

Every AI-generated knowledge article must have the status:

AI-SUGGESTED / DRAFT.

Never invent university policies, fees, dates, deadlines, examination rules, or official decisions.

If information is missing, mark it as unavailable.

## STAGE 04 — KNOWLEDGE VERIFIER AGENT

Role: Accuracy and Quality Control.

This stage is extremely important.

Responsibilities:

- Compare generated articles with original documents.
- Verify dates and deadlines.
- Check numerical values.
- Check fee amounts.
- Check scholarship amounts.
- Check academic requirements.
- Verify that the article does not introduce unsupported claims.
- Check references and source locations.
- Identify outdated information.
- Identify conflicting policies.
- Detect missing information.
- Check whether access permissions are appropriate.

Generate a verification report containing:

- Article title.
- Verification status.
- Source documents.
- Verified statements.
- Unsupported statements.
- Missing information.
- Conflicting information.
- Review warnings.

If an issue is found:

1. Return the article to Knowledge Curator.
2. Explain the exact problem.
3. Allow a maximum of TWO revision rounds.
4. Verify the corrected article again.
5. If unresolved, mark it ACTION REQUIRED.

Do not hide verification problems.

Do not guarantee zero hallucinations. Instead, make the system evidence-grounded and transparent about uncertainty.

## STAGE 05 — HUMAN REVIEWER

This is a human governance stage, not an AI agent.

The Human Reviewer must receive:

- AI-generated article.
- Original source document.
- Verification report.
- Extracted information.
- Warnings and conflicts.
- Revision history.

Provide four actions:

1. Approve.
2. Reject.
3. Request Changes.
4. Save as Pending.

After approval:

- Publish the article.
- Add its approved version to the Knowledge Library.
- Make it available to authorized users.
- Record the reviewer, date, and approval decision.

Never publish an AI-generated article automatically.

Never allow an unapproved article to appear in the normal user-facing Knowledge Assistant.

